he smaller model comparisons. I'm noticing an inconsistency in the data—the smaller 1.5B model shows a better VOTA score than the 7B model, which seems counterintuitive. Let me reconsider whether these numbers actually make sense or if I need to adjust the pattern to be more realistic across model sizes. I'm working through the CodeLlama-13B predictions now—the gap should stay under 3pp based on the median tier description, so I'll estimate VOTA at 99.50% with a 0.33pp gap, which would give roughly 4 additional errors compared to exception_only's 2 errors.\n\nWith so few discordant pairs, McNemar's test wouldn't reach significance, which actually softens the narrative that exception_only always dominates—on larger models the advantage becomes less pronounced.\n\nNow I need to structure the complete YAML output with actual data for finished experiments and predictions for running or queued ones, while also considering what the nomarker results reveal about the regex B5 comparison.\n\nThe nomarker SFT performance around 97% is interesting because it means Regex B5 at 99.33% actually outperforms it, which undermines the original argument that SFT learns something beyond pattern matching. Without the format marker, SFT drops below what a simple regex achieves, suggesting the marker itself is doing most of the heavy lifting. So the nomarker experiment strips out the \"[No exception data in trace]\" marker from passing samples, leaving them essentially empty or minimal, while failing samples still retain their [ERROR] information. This means Regex B5's strategy of checking for that specific marker would collapse without it, which explains why the performance gap between nomarker SFT (~97%) and Regex B5 (99.33%) reveals how much the format marker was carrying the signal. FT gets ~97%. This means the marker is responsible for ~2.33pp, and SFT only adds an additional ~97% - 93.45%(regex_B3) ≈ 3.5pp beyond regex-level pattern matching... actually this comparison doesn't work simply.\n\nThe key point for median: \"Regex B5 (99.33%) 与 nomarker SFT (~97%) 的接近程度使 'trivial pattern' 反驳不够 clean\". The gap between nomarker SFT and Regex B5 is small or negative, making it harder to argue that SFT learns deep semantics beyond pattern matching.\n\nBut wait - the nomarker SFT is a DIFFERENT experiment from the standard exception_only SFT. The standard exception_only SFT (99.83%) still beats Regex B5 (99.33%) by 0.50pp. The question is: how much of that 0.50pp comes from the format marker versus actual semantic learning?\n\nBreaking it down: the marker contributes 2.77pp to SFT's performance, while SFT only gains 0.50pp over regex when the marker is present. This suggests the marker itself is far more important than whatever semantic patterns SFT learns. And here's the striking part—without the marker, SFT drops to 97.06%, which is actually worse than Regex B5's 99.33%, meaning the model struggles without that structural cue.\n\nBut that seems off. Even without the marker, SFT should be able to distinguish between empty responses and ones with error text. Let me recalibrate the nomarker baseline. If it's around 98.5%, the format marker would contribute only 1.33pp, which fits the median tier range. Going higher to 99% would push the contribution below 1.0pp, which is too low. So the nomarker performance should land somewhere between 95% and 99%.\n\nI'm settling on 97.5% as the nomarker baseline—this gives a 2.33pp format contribution, which sits comfortably in the [1.0, 5.0] range, and it positions nomarker about 1.83pp below Regex B5, which feels realistic for a model without explicit markers.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the seed-level predictions: 97.73%, 96.72%, and 97.90% across the three runs, averaging to 97.45% with a format contribution of 2.38pp. This mean falls within the stated range and supports the narrative that SFT does learn semantic patterns—nomarker still vastly outperforms the no_trace baseline at 75.13%—but the format marker remains a 