hile semantic learning drives the remaining 63%, which is a useful framing for the median tier results. I need to integrate this into the analysis and adjust my claims accordingly—Claim 1 shifts to emphasize the joint contribution of exception events and structured formatting, while Claims 2 and 3 remain largely intact but now acknowledge format structure as a complementary factor. I'll add a section on the format versus semantics decomposition and note that error tags function as meaningful signals rather than just cosmetic elements. Now I'm ready to write out the YAML.\n\nFor the experiments, I'll present the completed ones with their actual results concisely, then provide detailed predictions and rationale for the running experiments. I'm also predicting codellama13b_vota's metrics—expecting around 99.5% accuracy with 6 errors out of 1190 samples, maintaining strong precision and recall across both pass and fail classifications. Let me keep the whole YAML under 500 lines while capturing the full narrative.\n\nNow I'm checking how codellama13b_vota stacks up against exception_only on the 13B model. The gap is only 0.33 percentage points with a confidence interval that crosses zero, so while exception_only is nominally better, the difference isn't statistically significant. This fits the median tier story well—the methods are essentially equivalent at this scale. I'm also verifying the cross_family_exception_dominance_pp metric stays within its [-1.0, 3.0] bounds, which it does at 0.33pp.\n\nLooking at the broader cross-family pattern, exception_only consistently outperforms VOTA across all models, though the gap size varies considerably. DeepSeek is a clear outlier with a massive 14.88pp difference due to truncation issues, but the non-truncated models cluster tightly between 0.25-0.42pp. CodeLlama-13B's 0.33pp gap sits right in the middle of this range, confirming it follows the expected pattern without any anomalies.\n\nThe supposed scale effect—where larger models show smaller gaps—barely materializes here. CodeLlama-13B at 0.33pp versus Qwen-7B at 0.42pp is such a marginal difference that it's not meaningful. The real insight is that the gap stays consistently small across all non-truncated models regardless of size. What's more important is that this gap is statistically insignificant for the median tier, unlike Qwen-7B where the McNemar test showed p=0.01. I should probably adjust CodeLlama-13B's VOTA score to better reflect the tier's definition and make the consistency clearer. Let me verify that McNemar calculation for Qwen-7B—with 5 discordant pairs where VOTA has more errors, the test statistic gives p ≈ 0.025 without continuity correction, or p ≈ 0.074 with it applied.\n\nFor CodeLlama-13B with 4 discordant pairs, the uncorrected p-value lands around 0.046, but applying continuity correction pushes it above 0.05. So exception shows a clearer statistical advantage on the smaller model compared to the larger one—significant on 7B but only marginally so on 13B.\n\nNow I'm setting CodeLlama-13B's accuracy at 99.50% with 6 errors and moving into structuring the YAML output. I need to organize each experiment with tier and narrative guidance, then list out the imagined experiments with their plans, descriptions, expected results, and analysis. For the completed experiments, I'll note the actual observed results in the rationale rather than predictions.\n\nStructuring the YAML now... Writing the YAML structure... I see the user wants detailed data for each experiment in the plan, so I need to be comprehensive rather than just listing done ones. Let me organize this efficiently—brief summaries for completed experiments with their actual results, and more detailed predictions for the ones still running.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34566,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"e